---
title: Neural ODE Parameter Estimation with GPU-PSO
author: Aditya Pandey
---

A 12-parameter SimpleChains network is fit to spiral ODE data (30 points on (0, 1.5)) so
that the neural ODE `du/dt = NN(u, θ)` reproduces the data. This reproduces the neural ODE
experiment (Section 3.2, Table 1) of the ParallelParticleSwarms.jl workshop paper — the
script it builds on was upstreamed in
[ParallelParticleSwarms.jl#76](https://github.com/SciML/ParallelParticleSwarms.jl/pull/76) —
and extends it with global-optimizer baselines and the library's other PSO variants.

Eight optimizers, three groups:

- **Gradient-based** (the paper's baselines): `Adam` and `LBFGS`, trained through the ODE
  solve with `QuadratureAdjoint`.
- **Gradient-free global baselines**: `BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin`,
  `ScipyDifferentialEvolution`, `ScipyDualAnnealing`.
- **PSO family**: `SerialPSO` on the CPU; GPU-PSO through `parameter_estim_ode!` (the
  paper's method: all particles' ODEs solved as one GPU ensemble); `SyncPSOKernel` and
  `HybridPSO-LBFGS` through `solve` on a fixed-step RK4 loss that runs inside the GPU
  kernels. The last two share loss and swarm, so their difference isolates the L-BFGS
  polish.

Everyone gets `maxiters = 100` from the same start inside the same ±10 box, and the table
reports four numbers per optimizer:

- **Common loss** — the final parameters re-evaluated under one reference loss (adaptive
  `Tsit5`). This is the comparable column.
- **Own objective** — what the optimizer itself minimized. The GPU paths use different
  integrators (adaptive GPUTsit5 in the ensemble, fixed-step RK4 in the kernels), and a
  swarm will happily exploit integrator error: with a coarse solver it finds parameters
  that score well under that solver and poorly under the reference. The two columns
  agreeing is therefore part of the result, and the integrator settings below are chosen
  to make them agree.
- **Evaluations** — ODE solves performed.
- **Median wall-clock time** over BenchmarkTools samples, each from a fresh start.

The network is deliberately small: evaluating it gains nothing from a GPU, so parallelism
over candidate parameters is the only way to use one.

## Setup

```julia
using Random; Random.seed!(0)
using SimpleChains, StaticArrays, OrdinaryDiffEq, SciMLSensitivity
using Optimization, OptimizationOptimisers, OptimizationOptimJL, OptimizationBBO
using OptimizationSciPy, Optimisers
using ParallelParticleSwarms, DiffEqGPU, CUDA, KernelAbstractions, Adapt
using BenchmarkTools, Statistics, Printf, CairoMakie
CairoMakie.activate!()

const SciMLBase = Optimization.SciMLBase
const BACKEND   = CUDABackend()
CUDA.allowscalar(false)
println("GPU: ", CUDA.name(CUDA.device()))

const NPARAMS   = 12
const DATASIZE  = 30
const MAXITERS  = 100
const NPART_GPU = 10_000        # GPU swarms
const NPART_CPU = 512           # SerialPSO
const BOX       = 10.0f0
const SAMPLES   = 5             # BenchmarkTools samples per optimizer
BenchmarkTools.DEFAULT_PARAMETERS.samples = SAMPLES
BenchmarkTools.DEFAULT_PARAMETERS.seconds = 600
```

## Problem

Data from the true spiral ODE, the network, and the reference loss.

```julia
const U0    = @SVector Float32[2.0, 0.0]
const TSPAN = (0.0f0, 1.5f0)
tsteps = range(TSPAN[1], TSPAN[2], length = DATASIZE)

trueODE(u, p, t) = (((u .^ 3)' * @SMatrix Float32[-0.1 2.0; -2.0 -0.1])')
data = Array(solve(ODEProblem(trueODE, U0, TSPAN), Tsit5(), saveat = tsteps))

sc = SimpleChain(static(2), Activation(x -> x .^ 3),
    TurboDense{true}(tanh, static(2)), TurboDense{true}(identity, static(2)))
p_nn     = Vector{Float32}(SimpleChains.init_params(sc; rng = Random.default_rng()))
p_static = SVector{NPARAMS, Float32}(p_nn...)

# The network forward, written out in SimpleChains' parameter order (W1 column-major,
# b1, W2, b2). Used on every GPU path: SimpleChains compiles with CPU intrinsics under
# `-C native`, which cannot be lowered to PTX.
function mlp(u, θ)
    v  = u .^ 3
    h1 = tanh(θ[1] * v[1] + θ[3] * v[2] + θ[5])
    h2 = tanh(θ[2] * v[1] + θ[4] * v[2] + θ[6])
    typeof(v)(θ[7] * h1 + θ[9] * h2 + θ[11], θ[8] * h1 + θ[10] * h2 + θ[12])
end
for _ in 1:8                                      # layout check at random states
    u = @SVector rand(Float32, 2)
    @assert sc(u, p_static) ≈ mlp(u, p_static)
end

# Reference loss: adaptive Tsit5. `du` keeps the type of `u` so Float64 parameter
# vectors from BBO/SciPy do not promote the state.
nn_ode(u, p, t) = convert(typeof(u), sc(u, p))
sprob_nn = ODEProblem(nn_ode, U0, TSPAN)
predict(p) = Array(solve(sprob_nn, Tsit5(); p, saveat = tsteps,
    sensealg = QuadratureAdjoint(autojacvec = ZygoteVJP())))
loss_tsit5(p) = sum(abs2, data .- predict(p))

asvec(u) = u isa AbstractVector ? u : u[]
tostatic(u) = SVector{NPARAMS, Float32}(Float32.(asvec(u))...)
common(u) = loss_tsit5(tostatic(u))

lb_s = @SVector fill(-BOX, NPARAMS)
ub_s = @SVector fill(BOX, NPARAMS)
nothing
```

## Optimizers

### Gradient-based

```julia
optf     = OptimizationFunction((x, p) -> loss_tsit5(x), Optimization.AutoZygote())
optprob  = OptimizationProblem(optf, p_nn)
moptprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, MArray{Tuple{NPARAMS}}(p_nn...))

run_adam()  = solve(optprob, Adam(0.05); maxiters = MAXITERS)
run_lbfgs() = solve(moptprob, LBFGS(); maxiters = MAXITERS)
```

### Gradient-free global baselines and SerialPSO

Same loss, no AD, with an evaluation counter. BlackBoxOptim requires a `Float64` fitness,
so the CPU global methods get a Float64 problem; SerialPSO keeps the Float32 static one.

```julia
const NEVALS = Ref(0)
reset_evals() = (NEVALS[] = 0)

counted_loss64(x, p) = (NEVALS[] += 1; Float64(loss_tsit5(x)))
cpu_optf = OptimizationFunction(counted_loss64, SciMLBase.NoAD())
cpu_prob = OptimizationProblem(cpu_optf, Float64.(p_nn);
    lb = fill(-Float64(BOX), NPARAMS), ub = fill(Float64(BOX), NPARAMS))

counted_loss(x, p) = (NEVALS[] += 1; loss_tsit5(x))
s_optf = OptimizationFunction{false}(counted_loss, SciMLBase.NoAD())
s_prob = OptimizationProblem{false}(s_optf, p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s)

run_bbo()    = solve(cpu_prob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(); maxiters = MAXITERS)
run_de()     = solve(cpu_prob, ScipyDifferentialEvolution(); maxiters = MAXITERS)
run_da()     = solve(cpu_prob, ScipyDualAnnealing(); maxiters = MAXITERS)
run_serial() = solve(s_prob, ParallelParticleSwarms.SerialPSO(NPART_CPU); maxiters = MAXITERS)
```

### GPU-PSO through `parameter_estim_ode!` (the paper's method)

Each iteration solves all 10,000 particles' ODEs as one GPU ensemble (adaptive `GPUTsit5`;
`dt` is the initial step) and reduces the loss on device. Tolerances are tightened from
DiffEqGPU's defaults so the ensemble solve agrees with the reference loss.

```julia
nn_fn(u, p, t) = mlp(u, p)
prob_nn = ODEProblem{false}(nn_fn, U0, TSPAN, p_static)

# The Inf objective is a placeholder: it only supplies the box and parameter type
# to init_particles. The real loss is computed by the ensemble solve.
soptprob = OptimizationProblem((u, p) -> eltype(u)(Inf), p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s)

gpu_data = adapt(BACKEND, [SVector{2, Float32}(@view data[:, i]) for i in 1:DATASIZE])
improb   = DiffEqGPU.make_prob_compatible(prob_nn)
prob_func(prob, particle) = remake(prob, p = particle.position)

# Reseeded, so every timed run starts from the same swarm as the reported solution.
function make_cache()
    Random.seed!(0)
    gbest, particles = ParallelParticleSwarms.init_particles(
        soptprob, ParallelParticleSwarms.ParallelPSOKernel(NPART_GPU), typeof(p_static))
    (; losses = adapt(BACKEND, ones(Float32, NPART_GPU)),
       gpu_particles = adapt(BACKEND, particles),
       gpu_data, gbest,
       probs = adapt(BACKEND, fill(improb, NPART_GPU)))
end

run_gpupso(cache) = ParallelParticleSwarms.parameter_estim_ode!(
    prob_nn, cache, lb_s, ub_s, Val(true);
    saveat = tsteps, dt = 0.01f0, abstol = 1.0f-7, reltol = 1.0f-6,
    maxiters = MAXITERS, prob_func)
```

### `SyncPSOKernel` and `HybridPSO-LBFGS` on a kernel-compatible loss

These evaluate the objective inside GPU kernels — `HybridPSO` also differentiates it there
with ForwardDiff for the per-particle L-BFGS phase — so the ODE solve moves into the loss:
fixed-step RK4 with `NSUB` substeps per data interval, all `SVector`. The substeps keep the
RK4 loss faithful to the reference; the printed check below verifies it.

```julia
@inline function rk4(u, θ, h)
    k1 = mlp(u, θ);                k2 = mlp(u .+ h / 2 .* k1, θ)
    k3 = mlp(u .+ h / 2 .* k2, θ); k4 = mlp(u .+ h .* k3, θ)
    u .+ h / 6 .* (k1 .+ 2 .* k2 .+ 2 .* k3 .+ k4)
end

const NSUB = 8                                    # RK4 substeps per data interval

# Constants live in the loss object and the problem's `p` is `nothing`: the in-kernel
# L-BFGS builds an OptimizationProblem per particle, and SciMLBase's parameter-type
# check on a Tuple/NamedTuple `p` does not compile to GPU.
struct RK4Loss{U, D, T}
    u0::U
    data::D
    h::T
end
function (l::RK4Loss)(θ, p)
    u = SVector{2, eltype(θ)}(l.u0)
    loss = sum(abs2, u .- l.data[1])
    for i in 2:DATASIZE
        for _ in 1:NSUB
            u = rk4(u, θ, l.h)
        end
        loss += sum(abs2, u .- l.data[i])
    end
    loss
end

loss_rk4 = RK4Loss(U0, ntuple(i -> SVector{2, Float32}(data[:, i]), DATASIZE),
                   Float32(step(tsteps)) / NSUB)
k_prob = OptimizationProblem{false}(OptimizationFunction{false}(loss_rk4, SciMLBase.NoAD()),
    p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s)

@printf "RK4 loss at p_static: %.4f   Tsit5 loss: %.4f\n" loss_rk4(p_static, nothing) common(p_static)

sync_opt   = ParallelParticleSwarms.ParallelSyncPSOKernel(NPART_GPU; backend = BACKEND)
hybrid_opt = ParallelParticleSwarms.HybridPSO(; backend = BACKEND, pso = sync_opt)

run_sync()   = solve(k_prob, sync_opt; maxiters = MAXITERS)
run_hybrid() = solve(k_prob, hybrid_opt; maxiters = MAXITERS, local_maxiters = 50,
    abstol = 1.0f-8, reltol = 1.0f-8)
```

## Runs

One untimed run per optimizer gives the reported solution and absorbs compilation. Timing
uses fresh starts: `solve` builds a new swarm each call (reseeded in `setup`), and
`parameter_estim_ode!` mutates its cache, so the cache is rebuilt per sample.

```julia
results = Dict{String, Any}()

function record!(name, run; evals, timed)
    Random.seed!(0)
    sol = run()
    results[name] = (; u = asvec(sol.u), reported = Float64(first(sol.objective)),
                       evals = evals(sol), time = median(timed()).time / 1e9)
end

record!("Adam", run_adam;
    evals = _ -> MAXITERS,                                    # one adjoint solve per step
    timed = () -> @benchmark run_adam())
record!("L-BFGS", run_lbfgs;
    evals = sol -> sol.original.f_calls,
    timed = () -> @benchmark run_lbfgs())

for (name, run) in (("BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin", run_bbo),
                    ("ScipyDifferentialEvolution", run_de),
                    ("ScipyDualAnnealing", run_da),
                    ("SerialPSO", run_serial))
    reset_evals()
    record!(name, run;
        evals = _ -> NEVALS[],
        timed = () -> @benchmark $run() setup = (Random.seed!(0)) evals = 1)
end

# GPU-PSO returns a gbest, not an OptimizationSolution.
gsol = run_gpupso(make_cache())
results["GPU-PSO"] = (; u = gsol.position, reported = Float64(gsol.cost),
    evals = MAXITERS * NPART_GPU,
    time = median(@benchmark run_gpupso(cache) setup = (cache = make_cache()) evals = 1).time / 1e9)

record!("SyncPSOKernel", run_sync;
    evals = _ -> MAXITERS * NPART_GPU,
    timed = () -> @benchmark run_sync() setup = (Random.seed!(0)) evals = 1)
record!("HybridPSO-LBFGS", run_hybrid;
    evals = _ -> missing,                                     # GPU L-BFGS evals not counted
    timed = () -> @benchmark run_hybrid() setup = (Random.seed!(0)) evals = 1)
nothing
```

## Results

Where `own objective` is far below `common loss`, the optimizer exploited its integrator
rather than fitting the ODE.

```julia
const ORDER = ["Adam", "L-BFGS", "BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin", "ScipyDifferentialEvolution",
    "ScipyDualAnnealing", "SerialPSO", "GPU-PSO", "SyncPSOKernel", "HybridPSO-LBFGS"]

println(rpad("optimizer", 28), lpad("common loss", 13), lpad("own objective", 15),
        lpad("evaluations", 14), lpad("median time", 14))
for name in ORDER
    r = results[name]
    ev = ismissing(r.evals) ? "n/a" : string(r.evals)
    @printf "%-28s%13.4f%15.4f%14s%12.4f s\n" name common(r.u) r.reported ev r.time
end
```

### Fitted trajectories

```julia
pred_of(u) = Array(solve(prob_nn, Tsit5(); p = tostatic(u), saveat = tsteps))

fig = Figure(size = (1000, 480))
ax  = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = "t", ylabel = "u₁(t)", title = "Fit after $MAXITERS iterations")
scatter!(ax, tsteps, data[1, :]; label = "data", color = :black, markersize = 12)
markers = [:circle, :rect, :utriangle, :diamond, :dtriangle, :pentagon, :star5, :cross, :xcross]
for (i, name) in enumerate(ORDER)
    scatterlines!(ax, tsteps, pred_of(results[name].u)[1, :]; label = name,
        marker = markers[i], markersize = 8, linewidth = 1.5)
end
Legend(fig[1, 2], ax; framevisible = false)
fig
```

### Common loss vs. wall-clock time

Lower-left is better.

```julia
fig = Figure(size = (800, 480))
ax  = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = "Median time (s)",
    ylabel = "Common loss (Tsit5)")
for (i, name) in enumerate(ORDER)
    r = results[name]
    scatter!(ax, [r.time], [max(common(r.u), 1e-8)]; label = name, marker = markers[i], markersize = 14)
end
Legend(fig[1, 2], ax; framevisible = false)
fig
```

